  "correction_id": "A-COR-0003",
  "rule_id": "A-R-005",
  "note": "Send it to Marv (owner) now rather than waiting a week; with no sales batch, there's nothing to match against, and a wrong match is worse than an escalation. Also, our threshold is $15, not $13.38: if the payout is short of the settlement report by up to $15, write it off to 6990, with fees to 6120. Anything over $15 goes to the owner, and our policy doesn't say anything about booking to 1300.",
  "explanation": "The controller confirmed that TapVend and SpinPay settlements with no sales batch go to the owner straight away. A-R-005 already catches them, because A-R-003 and A-R-004 both need a batch before they apply. So the escalation logic stays as it is.\n\nTwo things change:\n- **A-R-004:** the write-off limit goes from 13.38 to 15. Differences up to $15 are written off to 6990, with the report fees booked to 6120.\n- **A-R-005:** the threshold becomes \"more than $15\", and the instruction to book the shortfall to 1300 is removed, since their policy doesn't say that.\n\nThe controller stated both as policy, so I've also marked both rules approved.\n\nOne gap: the controller said \"short of the report by up to $15\". The rule language can only check the size of the gap, not which direction it goes. So an overpayment of $15 or less would also be written off under A-R-004. I've left it that way because the old rule already worked like this, but it may be worth checking with them."
 }
